===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|bere}}, from {{inh|en|ang|bēor|t=beer}} ({{w|Oxford English Dictionary|Oxford OED}} notes: "rare, except in [[poetry]]"), from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*beuʀ}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*beuzą|t=beer}} (putatively from Proto-Indo-European *bʰeusóm), meaning “brewer's yeast”. 

However, also see the [https://www.etymonline.com/word/beer#etymonline_v_8236 "beer" entry on OED] (''[[q.v.]]''), which links a connection to monastic {{der|en|VL.|*biber||a drink, beverage}}, from {{der|en|la|bibere||to drink}}. {{w|Samuel Johnson}} in his famous 18th-century ''{{w|A Dictionary of the English Language}}'' guessed it was from (unattested) {{noncog|cy|*bîr}}; he distinguished it in his time from {{m|en|ale}} — the ancient usual word — by beer being older-aged and/or smaller. 

Cognate with {{cog|stq|Bjoor}}, {{cog|fy|bier}}, {{cog|nds-de|Beer}}, {{cog|nl|bier}}, {{cog|de|Bier}}, {{cog|is|bjór|t=beer}}.

